// @check
// @error `u8[]` cannot be passed to a generic parameter
// @error `u8[]` cannot be passed to a generic parameter
// @error `u8[]` cannot be passed to a generic parameter
// @error `u8[]` cannot be passed to a generic parameter
// A `u8[]` MEETING A GENERIC PARAMETER, in the DIRECT and the UFCS spelling of the same
// call, side by side. `T` ranges over VALUE types and `u8` is storage, so `u8[]` is not an
// instantiation of `T[]` — the rule is a consequence of the type being honest rather than a
// hole in the generics, and both spellings must state it identically.
//
// THE PAIRING IS THE POINT. The rule reached only the direct spelling for as long as it
// existed: it lives in the argument loop, and `self` does not travel through an argument
// loop — a UFCS receiver arrives ahead of one. So `indexOf(xs, 2)` was a clean checker error
// while `xs.indexOf(2)` was `vl check` rc 0 over an INVALID MODULE — the worst class this
// corpus tracks, on the same export and the same receiver. A future rung that lands on one
// spelling and not the other fails this file rather than hiding behind its twin.
//
// `bindGenWalk` declines to bind `T := u8`, so the substituted parameter keeps its unsolved
// hole — and `assignable` answers TRUE for a hole. That is why this needs its own message
// instead of falling out of the ordinary argument compare, on BOTH sides.
import { indexOf } from "std:array"

const xs: u8[] = [1, 2, 3]

print(indexOf(xs, 2))
print(xs.indexOf(2))

// A hand-written generic importing nothing — the same rule, off the std surface, so a
// reading of this file cannot conclude the restriction is something `std:array` chose.
function firstOf<T>(self: T[]): T { return self[0] }

print(firstOf(xs))
print(xs.firstOf())
